Establish the stability regions of a symmetric nonideal mixture (@chemistry-phase-stability: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "../../engineering/materials-engineering/computation/An-inspectable-symmetric-phase-separation-account.lingua#r_1ZQTEHAPQAXJECBG9EBDQDJZGZ") { r_00F8769C54AFC34C6A67B474EB
Use the preceding shared regular-solution model, retaining its assumptions. After removing the common pure-component reference line, write g_mix=RT φ(x), with φ=x ln x+(1−x)ln(1−x)+χx(1−x), χ=Ω/(RT), 0≤x≤1. Take x ln x=0 at x=0 by its continuous limit. This is a stipulated symmetric bulk constitutive model. Keeping ideal configurational entropy while representing interactions by one composition-independent Ω is an approximation; the existence of microscopic interactions does not prove this formula. [Ideal mixing and the statistical origin of entropy](../../engineering/materials-engineering/thermodynamics/Ideal-mixing-and-the-statistical-origin-of-entropy.lingua#r_7D6PPV1WNXY44R21H9MN2Z42BQ) and [Specify microscopic states macroscopic questions and statistical premises](../../physics/core/Specify-microscopic-states-macroscopic-questions-and-statistical-premises.lingua#r_00CF73DE0F1F6E72DAF547A67B) supply the counting preparation and its limitations.

For 0<x<1, φ′=ln[x/(1−x)]+χ(1−2x) and φ″=1/[x(1−x)]−2χ. Since x(1−x)≤1/4, χ≤2 gives φ″≥0. At χ=2 it vanishes only at x=1/2; the derivative still increases strictly across every interval. No two distinct compositions lower the bulk free energy below the homogeneous value by mixing in this regime. This conclusion concerns this candidate curve, not every possible crystal or liquid phase of a real substance.

For χ>2, let s_±=(1±sqrt(1−2/χ))/2. Curvature is negative between them and positive outside. On (0,s_−), φ′ increases from −∞; on (s_−,1/2), it decreases to φ′(1/2)=0, hence φ′(s_−)>0. There is exactly one root a in (0,s_−). The function decreases before a and increases from a to 1/2. Symmetry gives a second minimum at b=1−a, with a central maximum at 1/2. Thus φ(a)=φ(b) is the global minimum on [0,1] and its horizontal line is supporting. The endpoints x=0,1 have value zero, larger than the minima because φ initially decreases from zero.

For a≤z≤b the two-phase mixture has molar mixing free energy RT φ(a). Outside that interval the homogeneous branch remains the lower envelope. To justify the latter claim, join the strictly convex outer pieces to the flat interior at zero slope. This joined function is convex, lies below φ and is attained by the homogeneous or two-phase construction at every z. Its supporting lines supply the same lower-bound proof as the preceding lesson. A finite grid is a check of calculations, not the proof over all compositions.

The boundaries a,b are binodal compositions. The s_± values are spinodal limits of local curvature. Between a and s_−, or s_+ and b, the homogeneous phase has positive curvature but a lower-energy finite separation exists: metastability in this bulk description. In the negative-curvature interval, splitting equal portions to z±δ changes their average φ by (φ″(z)/2)δ² plus higher-order terms, lowering it for sufficiently small δ. Interfacial and gradient terms, transport and nucleation determine actual finite-scale dynamics; none is a transformation-time prediction of this curve.

At χ=3, z=1/10 has φ″=46/9>0 but lies inside the coexistence interval. The shared solver will locate a near 0.07072018; the next new lesson supplies an independent certificate. A hypothetical additional fixed-composition phase at x=1/2 with reduced molar energy −1 would defeat this liquid pair there: φ(x)≥−ln2>−1 for χ=3. It is deliberately a rival candidate, not measured material information.

The corresponding pure-liquid-reference activities also follow by differentiation: ln γ_A=χx² and ln γ_B=χ(1−x)², so μ_A−μ_A*=RT[ln(1−x)+χx²] and μ_B−μ_B*=RT[ln x+χ(1−x)²]. The weighted fixed-T,p derivatives cancel as required by Gibbs–Duhem. Positive deviations from ideal activity do not alone imply phase separation; χ≤2 remains convex in this model. If Ω is temperature-independent and positive, the model critical temperature is Ω/(2R); fitting χ at one temperature does not establish that temperature dependence.

**Work:** prove the root/curvature classification, supporting envelope and activity formulas. Contrast a locally unstable, metastable and globally stable homogeneous composition. Explain why adding a common affine reference preserves coexistence, while a new phase or asymmetric interaction can change it. Continue through the unchanged shared solver, then certify its relevant output independently.

